"epic cards element" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		&lt;epic-cards&gt;
		<header>The &lt;epic-cards&gt; element: a saga page's Epics, a card per epic serving the saga, grouped by state.</>
	</>

	<text class="lede" paragraph><text code>&lt;epic-cards&gt;</> is a saga page's Epics: a card for each epic whose page names the saga, in the Epics page's groups. It asks the page server for them, so it is never out of date.</>
	<text class="meta" paragraph>
		Written for: anyone reading or writing a saga page's markup, or changing this element.

		Status: <label size="mini" color="green">built</> (epic <text code>epic-workflow</> P15), in the <link target="src-spell-epic-src-components-epic-cards" link="/epic/src/components/epic-cards/"><text title="epic/src/components/epic-cards/" code>epic-cards</></> folder of the epics package.

		Its vocabulary (attributes, parts, texts): <link target="src-spell-epic-src-components-epic-cards-epiccards-en-ts" link="/epic/src/components/epic-cards/EpicCards.en.ts"><text title="epic/src/components/epic-cards/EpicCards.en.ts" code>EpicCards.en.ts</></>.

		The component: <link target="src-spell-epic-src-components-epic-cards-epiccards-tsx" link="/epic/src/components/epic-cards/EpicCards.tsx"><text title="epic/src/components/epic-cards/EpicCards.tsx" code>EpicCards.tsx</></>.
	</>

	<section label="1. Example">
		<icon slot="icon" name="eye"/>
		<example "Example">
			<html>
				<epic-cards saga="epics"></epic-cards>
			</html>
		</example>
		<text paragraph>The Epic Tooling saga's epics, live from this page server. Opened from disk, it says it needs the page server instead; with no epic naming the saga yet, it says how to name one.</>
	</>

	<section label="2. Markup">
		<icon slot="icon" name="code"/>
		<text paragraph>The saga page template's Epics:</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<epic-section id="epics" kind="epics">
			  <epic-cards saga="{{id}}"></epic-cards>
			</epic-section>
			```
		</>
		<text paragraph>From the tool's saga page template (<link target="src-spell-epic-src-tool-templates-saga-html" link="/epic/src/tool/templates/saga.html"><text title="epic/src/tool/templates/saga.html" code>saga.html</></>), which <text code>epic saga page &lt;id&gt;</> fills in. Nothing else goes in it: the cards come from the epic pages.</>
	</>

	<section label="3. Where it goes">
		<icon slot="icon" name="compass"/>
		<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
			<row header>
				<cell>Parent</>
				<cell>How</>
				<cell>How many</>
				<cell>As</>
			</>
			<row>
				<cell>
					<link target="src-packages-epics-docs-epic-section-html" link="/epic-section">
						<text code>&lt;epic-section&gt;</>
					</>
				</>
				<cell>
					<text code>kind="epics"</>
				</>
				<cell>one at most</>
				<cell>A saga page's Epics.</>
			</>
		</>
		<text paragraph>It has no children.</>
	</>

	<section label="4. Attributes">
		<icon slot="icon" name="table columns"/>
		<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
			<row header>
				<cell>Attribute</>
				<cell>Value</>
				<cell>What it is</>
			</>
			<row>
				<cell><text code>saga</> (required)</>
				<cell>a saga id</>
				<cell>The saga whose epics it shows: <text code>drawable-spell</>.</>
			</>
		</>

		<section label="Parts" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing open="false">
			<text paragraph>Pieces of what it draws, which a page's CSS can style from outside with <text code>::part(name)</>.</>
			<table celled padding="compact" appearance="striped" unstackable>
				<row header>
					<cell>Part</>
					<cell>What it is</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>base</>
					</>
					<cell>The box around the list.</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>list</>
					</>
					<cell>The cards, an <link target="src-spell-epic-src-docs-epic-list-html" link="/epic-list"><text code>&lt;epic-list no-favorites&gt;</></> of <link target="src-spell-epic-src-docs-epic-card-html" link="/epic-card"><text code>&lt;epic-card&gt;</></>s: the Epics page's groups and cards.</>
				</>
				<row>
					<cell>
						<text code>note</>
					</>
					<cell>Instead of cards: no epic names the saga yet, the page opened from disk, or no answer.</>
				</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="5. How it looks">
		<icon slot="icon" name="palette"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>The Epics page's own list and cards (epic <text code>epic-engine</> I39): an <link target="src-spell-epic-src-docs-epic-list-html" link="/epic-list"><text code>&lt;epic-list&gt;</></> of <link target="src-spell-epic-src-docs-epic-card-html" link="/epic-card"><text code>&lt;epic-card&gt;</></>s, so an epic looks and behaves the same on both pages: its mark, star, saga label and last-worked day.</>
			<item>Its groups, in the Epics page's order: Active, Planning, Urgent, Done. No Favorites: that's your own list, on the Epics page (<text code>no-favorites</>); a starred epic keeps its group, its star solid.</>
			<item>Each card: the title links to the epic page, in that epic's own tab; the summary, at most four lines; then the state, phases done of all, and the phase under way.</>
			<item>A grid of cards at least 260px wide: three to a row in an epic page's column, one in the side bar.</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="6. Written by">
		<icon slot="icon" name="terminal"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item><text code>epic saga page &lt;id&gt;</>: the saga page template writes it, once.</>
			<item>What it draws comes from the page server, <text code>GET /api/epics/saga?saga=&lt;id&gt;</> (<link target="src-spell-epic-src-tool-sagaepics-ts" link="/epic/src/tool/SagaEpics.ts"><text title="epic/src/tool/SagaEpics.ts" code>SagaEpics</></>): every epic page whose <text code>&lt;epic-page saga&gt;</> names it. It asks again whenever an epic page changes.</>
		</>
	</>
